#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084001} :activity/id #uuid "b6e2b9c9-b43f-56cb-bffb-ee087748f64d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048385 :topic/id #uuid "9b297cff-c3ae-5483-aeeb-eb227b91f17f" :topic/math-academy-id 752} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084001 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084420 :tutorial/content "Recall that a critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that \n\n- either $f'(c)=0$\n\n- or $f'(c)$ does not exist.\n\nFor example, let's find the critical points of the function\n\n$$\nf(x) = x^3- 12x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}-12x) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where $f'(c) = 0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-12 & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & =12 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x = \\pm 2.$\n\n- Second, we note that $f'(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ is $x= \\pm 2.$\n\n![](images/b8/b85f65df1a546b167919a3b9416bafca0cce68f92292fba671d619c75bd4c3b2.png)\n\nThese values correspond to the points $A$ and $B,$ as shown on the graph." :tutorial/id #uuid "ec16a557-b8b7-5dc1-a800-8546b77e9167" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2662 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "167e77b9-5b0a-5de6-8a1e-8cd6789a6e7d" :step/math-academy-id 12773 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084229}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084229 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083295 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083076 :question/id #uuid "5c0c0cf3-3d1c-5d6b-8835-d73c97e4cb39" :question/math-academy-id "e-6722"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4bd2a8e-357a